NAME
       grub2-mkconfig - generate the GRUB configuration file

SYNOPSIS
       grub2-mkconfig [-o FILE] [--update-bls-cmdline]

DESCRIPTION
       grub2-mkconfig builds GRUB's main configuration file from the settings
       in /etc/default/grub and the scripts in /etc/grub.d. Without -o it only
       prints the result, which is useful for checking but changes nothing.

       On RHEL 9 the kernels themselves are listed by the entries in
       /boot/loader/entries, which grub.cfg reads at boot. Settings such as
       GRUB_TIMEOUT go into grub.cfg, so after changing them you must run
       grub2-mkconfig -o. The kernel command line lives in each entry's
       "options" line: after changing GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX add
       --update-bls-cmdline so the entries get the new arguments too (or use
       grubby --update-kernel=ALL, which does both).

       The file to write is /boot/grub2/grub.cfg on both BIOS and UEFI
       machines. (On UEFI, /boot/efi/EFI/redhat/grub.cfg is a small stub that
       loads it: do not overwrite the stub.)

OPTIONS
       -o, --output=FILE
              Write the configuration to FILE instead of printing it.

       --update-bls-cmdline
              Also write the GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX arguments into the "options"
              line of every entry in /boot/loader/entries.

EXAMPLES
       Make the menu wait 10 seconds:
              # vim /etc/default/grub        (set GRUB_TIMEOUT=10)
              # grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg
              Generating grub configuration file ...
              done

       Add an argument for every kernel through /etc/default/grub:
              # vim /etc/default/grub        (add it to GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX)
              # grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg --update-bls-cmdline

FILES
       /etc/default/grub, /etc/grub.d/, /boot/grub2/grub.cfg,
       /boot/loader/entries/*.conf

SEE ALSO
       grubby(8), grub2-set-default(8), grub2-editenv(1)
